WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.624
Golden sets, regression sets, and versioning.

2
00:00:02.904 --> 00:00:07.512
Treat test sets like code, because they are part of your system’s specification.

3
00:00:07.792 --> 00:00:21.744
Store them in the repo as plain files (JSONL or YAML), one case per record with a stable id, the input, the expected behavior, the source (log, expert, synthetic), the failure mode it targets, and a short reason.

4
00:00:22.024 --> 00:00:25.224
Review changes in pull requests like any other change.

5
00:00:25.504 --> 00:00:26.421
Version them.

6
00:00:26.701 --> 00:00:31.075
When you add, remove, or relabel cases, bump a version and record why.

7
00:00:31.355 --> 00:00:42.320
Every eval result should record which set version it ran on, alongside the prompt and model versions; a score from v3 of the set is not comparable to a score from v4.

8
00:00:42.600 --> 00:00:51.261
The evals FAQ discusses updating a gold dataset as the product changes, and that update is exactly when versioning earns its keep.

9
00:00:51.541 --> 00:00:55.595
Never edit a case to make a failure go away without a recorded reason.

10
00:00:55.875 --> 00:01:00.248
Sometimes the expected answer really was wrong (an expert should confirm).

11
00:01:00.528 --> 00:01:02.256
Sometimes the product changed.

12
00:01:02.536 --> 00:01:04.029
Both are fine with a note.

13
00:01:04.309 --> 00:01:08.981
Quietly loosening a case because a release is due is how a regression set rots.

14
00:01:09.261 --> 00:01:10.648
Graduate and retire.

15
00:01:10.928 --> 00:01:14.021
Regression cases stay as long as the behavior matters.

16
00:01:14.301 --> 00:01:26.504
When a capability eval’s pass rate gets high, Anthropic’s post suggests promoting its cases into the regression suite and adding harder ones, since a saturated set no longer tells you how to improve.
